      "summary_markdown": "- **Made_sla is driven mainly by reassignment_count** \u2014 Among the drivers tested, reassignment_count explains the most of made_sla; reassignment_count has 0.7x the odds.\n- **Resolution hours is higher for opened after hours = true** \u2014 Median resolution hours is 51.7 for opened after hours = true versus 33.6 for false.\n- **Missed SLA concentrates in reassignment count = 3+** \u2014 Records with reassignment count = 3+ have a missed SLA rate of 29.4% versus 9.6% for reassignment count = 1 (3.1x).\n- **Volume is concentrated in few cmdb ci name values** \u2014 Payments Gateway accounts for 39.0% of records (where priority = 1).\n- **Priority concentrates in assignment group name = Application Support** \u2014 Records with assignment group name = Application Support have a priority rate of 5.9% versus 2.5% for assignment group name = Network Operations (2.3x). The effect only just clears the minimum size that counts, so this is weak evidence. It is not yet confirmed on separate data: treat it as a lead worth checking, not an established fact.\n- **Priority concentrates in cmdb_ci_name = Payments Gateway** \u2014 Records with cmdb_ci_name = Payments Gateway have a priority rate of 30.0% versus 1.9% for cmdb_ci_name = CRM (15.9x).\n- **Missed SLA concentrates in category = network** \u2014 Records with category = network have a missed SLA rate of 19.2% versus 12.2% for category = database (1.6x).\n- **Priority concentrates in category = software** \u2014 Records with category = software have a priority rate of 5.8% versus 2.6% for category = network (2.2x). The effect only just clears the minimum size that counts, so this is weak evidence. It is not yet confirmed on separate data: treat it as a lead worth checking, not an established fact.\n\n_Not yet confirmed on separate data, so treat these as leads worth checking, not established facts: I-5, I-8._\n_Weak evidence (the effect only just clears the minimum size that counts): I-5, I-8._",
